# Build context exclusions for deploy/Dockerfile.
#
# One image builds from this directory as the context, and Docker reads
# .dockerignore only from the context root — never from beside the Dockerfile
# and never from a subdirectory. web/ therefore cannot carry its own; the
# dashboard still gets built here (the node stage produces the bundle the Go
# stage embeds), so the web/-prefixed block below is still the only thing
# excluding its artifacts.
#
# Patterns are anchored here and a single * does not cross /, so a rule for a
# file inside web/ has to say web/ explicitly. `.env` does not cover
# `web/.env`.

.git
.github
.gitignore
.env
.env.*
*.md
!README.md
docs/
test/
*_test.go
**/*_test.go
.vscode
.idea
tmp/
vendor/

# The dashboard bundle the gateway embeds.
#
# Not an input: the node stage builds it and `COPY --from` writes it into
# place, so sending a locally built copy would only invalidate the internal/
# layer every time someone runs the frontend build. Excluding the committed
# placeholder index.html with it is deliberate and safe — nothing in the image
# should ever compile against the placeholder, and if the inter-stage copy were
# ever dropped the Go build would fail loudly on the empty embed pattern rather
# than ship a blank dashboard.
internal/webui/dist/

# ── Dashboard ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Regenerated by npm ci or the build.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
web/*.tsbuildinfo

# Test artifacts, and the browser suite the production build never reads.
web/coverage/
web/playwright-report/
web/test-results/
web/e2e/
#
# web/playwright.config.ts and web/tsconfig.e2e.json are deliberately NOT
# excluded. The root tsconfig references the latter as a project and
# `npm run build` runs `tsc -b`, which resolves every reference before
# compiling — dropping the file fails the build with TS5083.
#
# Keeping that file alone was not enough either. Its `include` is exactly
# ["e2e", "playwright.config.ts"], so excluding both left the project with no
# inputs and `tsc -b` failed with TS18003 instead — a different error in the
# same place. One of the two paths has to survive, and playwright.config.ts is
# the small one.

# Local environment overrides.
#
# Vite loads these during the build and inlines every VITE_-prefixed value into
# the published bundle, so an operator's local file would be baked into the
# image and served to every browser that loads the dashboard. This is the rule
# to be most careful with: the bare .env entries above do not reach into web/.
web/.env
web/.env.*
